'''eTamil''' is a [[high-level programming language|high-level]] [[programming language]] whose keywords are words of the [[Tamil language]]. It is designed as a [[domain-specific language]] for Indian [[accounting]], [[taxation in India|taxation]] and [[financial technology]], and also provides the features of a [[general-purpose programming language]], including first-class functions, user-defined record types, modules, database access and an [[Web server|HTTP server]].<ref name="readme" /><ref name="ictetl" />

Financial concepts are part of the language's vocabulary rather than a library: {{lang|ta|வரவு}} (credit), {{lang|ta|பற்று}} (debit), {{lang|ta|வரி}} (tax) and {{lang|ta|இருப்புநிலை}} (balance sheet) are keywords.<ref name="keywords" /> Every number is a [[Fixed-point arithmetic|fixed-point]] [[Decimal data type|decimal]], so that <code>0.1 + 0.2</code> evaluates to exactly <code>0.3</code>.<ref name="arch" /> Each keyword may be written in [[Tamil script]], in an [[ASCII]] romanization, or for most keywords with an English alias.<ref name="ictetl" /><ref name="keywords" />

eTamil was created by Esan Maruff. It first appeared on [[GitHub]] in June 2024, and its current implementation dates from January 2026.<ref name="repos" /><ref name="changelog" /> Its reference implementation is written in [[Rust (programming language)|Rust]] and includes a [[bytecode]] [[virtual machine]] and an experimental [[LLVM]] back end.<ref name="readme" /> Its [[standard library]], ''nUlakam'', is written in eTamil itself.<ref name="nulakam" />

== History ==
eTamil is the work of Esan Maruff (legal name Mohammed Maruff).<ref name="authors" /> It first appeared publicly on 26 June 2024 as eTml, a GitHub repository describing "an Indian DSL for Accounts & FinTech" with an early implementation in Rust.<ref name="repos" /> On 5 July 2024 it was followed by the eTamil repository, which held a prototype compiler, with a lexer, parser, semantic analyser and code generator, together with the project's website at etamil.in.<ref name="site-initial" />

Maruff enrolled as a PhD scholar in the Department of Computer Applications at [[B. S. Abdur Rahman Crescent Institute of Science and Technology]], Chennai, to document the language's approach, under the title "Developing an Indian DSL (Programming Language) for Accounts, Commerce, Finance, and Fintech Professionals".<ref name="citation" /><ref name="authors" /> A paper of the same title was presented at the International Conference on Technology Enhanced Teaching and Learning (ICTETL 2025), held online on 23–24 October 2025 by the institute's Centre for Distance and Online Education in collaboration with Global College Malta, and published in the conference proceedings.<ref name="ictetl" />

Over the following two years the language was developed in a series of separate repositories, each covering one part of the project or trying a different architecture. Among them were eTamil-ciRal, a framework of tools and libraries (August 2024); eTamil-nivaqi (September 2024); Accounts, an accounting module in Rust (February 2025); nivaqi-v1 (March 2025); and eTamil-Compiler, eTamil-vscode and eTamil_transpiler (January and February 2026).<ref name="repos" /> All were superseded by the current repository, first published on 26 January 2026 with a compiler and a [[Visual Studio Code]] extension providing syntax highlighting.<ref name="changelog" /> Version 0.2 followed on 31 January 2026, adding documentation for the language's transliteration scheme.<ref name="changelog" /> The original eTamil repository was not discarded, because it hosts the project's website; only its Rust compiler code was removed, in August 2026.<ref name="site-removal" />

The language was substantially reworked in August 2026. Binary [[floating-point arithmetic]] was replaced throughout by fixed-point decimals, the financial and accounting vocabulary grew from 157 to 196 keywords, and functions, arrays, records, iteration, [[result type]]s and modules were added, together with database access and an HTTP server.<ref name="changelog" /><ref name="roadmap" />

Version 1.0.0 was released on 13 September 2026, with prebuilt packages for Windows, Linux and macOS and a Visual Studio Code extension that bundles the full toolchain.<ref name="changelog" /> Versions 1.0.1 to 1.0.3 added a bundled typeface released under the [[SIL Open Font License]]. Version 1.1.0, released on 23 September 2026, added immutable bindings, [[first-class function]]s, record shapes and methods.<ref name="changelog" /> Version 1.2.0 added a package for the [[Raspberry Pi]] and other 64-bit ARM Linux systems, and made <code>.qmz</code> the only source-file extension; version 1.3.0 added compilation of programs to firmware for [[Arduino]] boards; and version 1.4.0 added a library for document retrieval and language models, written in eTamil. Version 1.4.2, released on 28 September 2026, carried security updates to two dependencies.<ref name="changelog" /> Version 2.0.0, released on RELEASE_DATE_TEXT_TODO, added libraries for retail banking, a [[Language Server Protocol|language server]] and plugins for several editors, a [[Project Jupyter|Jupyter]] kernel, and installation through package managers and containers, and published a specification of the language.<ref name="release-2" />

