== Design philosophy and features ==
eTamil's design documents state that the language should let Tamil-speaking accountants and programmers express financial logic in their own language, without being a translation of an English-keyword language.<ref name="readme" /> The project describes several fixed principles:<ref name="arch" />

* '''Exact money.''' Every value is a 28-digit fixed-point decimal and binary floating point is never used, so that a [[double-entry bookkeeping|double-entry]] ledger balances exactly. Division keeps full precision and rounding is explicit, following the Indian tax practice of rounding once at the end of a computation.
* '''Loud failure.''' Unsupported constructs, undefined variables, division by zero and out-of-range indices raise errors rather than producing a default value. The project's rationale is that a silently wrong result from a tax calculation is worse than a crash, because a successful run is not investigated.
* '''Errors as values.''' Following Rust, operations that can fail return {{lang|ta|சரி}} (success) or {{lang|ta|தவறு}} (failure), propagated with a <code>?</code> operator. The language has no [[exception handling|exceptions]].
* '''A thin host.''' Only what the language cannot express, such as system calls, database drivers and decimal and calendar primitives, is implemented in Rust. The standard library and the accounting framework are written in eTamil, which the project presents as evidence that the language is sufficient for the frameworks built on it.<ref name="arch" /><ref name="nulakam" />
* '''Names as written.''' An identifier is stored exactly as the programmer typed it, so a Tamil author's chosen names are never replaced by English equivalents.<ref name="keywords" />

Error messages are bilingual, in Tamil and English, and report a line and column. Columns count written Tamil letters rather than bytes.<ref name="readme" />

== Syntax and semantics ==
eTamil uses braces to delimit blocks and semicolons to end statements; these and much of the rest of its syntax were evaluated against [[C (programming language)|C]], [[C++]] and Rust before being finalised, and a few keywords were adopted from Ezhil.<ref name="readme" /><ref name="site-about" /> Unlike most brace-delimited languages, eTamil places a condition before its keyword: <code>(x > 0) எனில் { … }</code> reads as "(x > 0) if { … }".<ref name="readme" /> A specification of its lexical structure and grammar, including a grammar in [[Extended Backus–Naur form]], is published with the source code.<ref name="spec" />

=== Keywords and scripts ===
The language has 203 keywords, accepted in 545 spellings in total.<ref name="readme" /> Each keyword has a Tamil-script form and a romanized form in the project's ''ezuqqu'' scheme, which maps each Tamil letter to ASCII characters so that programs can be typed on an ordinary keyboard. The scheme keeps the three Tamil nasal consonants distinct: {{lang|ta|ண}} is written <code>N</code>, {{lang|ta|ந}} <code>n</code> and {{lang|ta|ன}} <code>Z</code>.<ref name="keywords" /><ref name="romanization" /> Most keywords also have an English alias beginning with an underscore, such as <code>_if</code>.<ref name="keywords" />

