Type keywords and SQL clause keywords are reserved. Financial keywords are not, so {{lang|ta|தொகை}} (amount) may be used as a variable name.<ref name="keywords" /> Because names are stored as typed, <code>வருவாய்</code> and its romanization <code>varuvAy</code> are different variables, although as keywords the two spellings are interchangeable.<ref name="keywords" />

=== Statements and control flow ===
eTamil's statements include:<ref name="readme" />
* assignment with <code>=</code>, and the {{lang|ta|நிலை}} statement, which binds a name once; reassigning it or changing an element or field of its value is rejected before the program runs;
* the {{lang|ta|எனில்}} and {{lang|ta|இன்றேல்}} conditional statements;
* the {{lang|ta|சுற்று}} loop, which repeats while its condition holds, and the {{lang|ta|ஒவ்வொரு}} ... {{lang|ta|இல்}} loop over arrays, records and strings;
* {{lang|ta|செயல்}} function definitions and the {{lang|ta|திரும்பு}} statement;
* {{lang|ta|இறக்கு}}, which imports a module, resolving the path beside the importing file and then along an <code>ETAMIL_PATH</code> [[environment variable]];
* {{lang|ta|வழி}}, which declares an HTTP route, and {{lang|ta|பதில்}}, which sends a response;
* {{lang|ta|இடைவெளி}}, which runs a block repeatedly at a fixed interval under the HTTP server.

=== Expressions ===
Arithmetic uses the usual infix operators. A number followed by <code>%</code> is a percentage, so <code>18%</code> is the value 0.18. Strings are concatenated with <code>&</code>.<ref name="readme" /> The logical operators {{lang|ta|மற்றும்}} (and) and {{lang|ta|அல்லது}} (or) [[Short-circuit evaluation|short-circuit]], and {{lang|ta|இல்லை}} is logical negation. Comparisons chain, so <code>a > b > c</code> is equivalent to testing each neighbouring pair.<ref name="roadmap" />

Arrays are written <code>[…]</code> and records <code>{…}</code>. Arrays compare equal element by element and records field by field.<ref name="roadmap" /> A function written where a value is expected, <code>செயல்(x) { திரும்பு x * 2; }</code>, is an anonymous function; one created inside another function captures copies of the local variables it uses.<ref name="changelog" />

=== Typing ===
eTamil is [[Dynamic typing|dynamically typed]], with optional type declarations that are enforced. A variable, parameter or return value may be declared as a number ({{lang|ta|எண்}}), string ({{lang|ta|சொல்}}) or function ({{lang|ta|செயல்}}). The checker is deliberately narrow and makes no claim about undeclared values, so that working programs are not rejected by guesses.<ref name="roadmap" />

A {{lang|ta|வடிவம்}} declares a record shape: its fields and the type each holds. A record made as that shape must supply every field and no others. Errors that can be seen in the source are reported before execution; values known only at run time, such as database rows, are checked when the record is made. Functions declared inside a shape are its methods. A method whose first parameter is {{lang|ta|இது}} ("this") is called on a record and cannot modify it, comparable to Rust's <code>&self</code>; a changed record is a new value. There is no [[Inheritance (object-oriented programming)|inheritance]].<ref name="changelog" />

=== Arithmetic operations ===
All numbers are fixed-point decimals with up to 28 significant digits; the language has no separate integer or floating-point types. Equality between numbers is exact.<ref name="readme" /><ref name="arch" /> Division retains full precision, and the built-in {{lang|ta|வட்டமிடு}} rounds to a given number of decimal places.<ref name="readme" /> When stored, money keeps its exactness: PostgreSQL columns use <code>NUMERIC</code>, MongoDB documents use <code>Decimal128</code>, and SQLite stores decimals as text.<ref name="readme" />

=== Function syntax ===
A function is declared with {{lang|ta|செயல்}}, a name, a parenthesised parameter list and a block. Parameters and the return value may carry declared types. Functions support recursion and local scope, and are values: they may be stored in variables, arrays and records, passed as arguments and returned.<ref name="changelog" /><ref name="readme" />

== Code examples ==
[[Hello, world|"Hello, World!" program]]:

<syntaxhighlight lang="text">
அச்சு "வணக்கம், உலகம்!";
</syntaxhighlight>

Exact decimal arithmetic, with the output of each line shown in a comment:

<syntaxhighlight lang="text">
அச்சு 0.1 + 0.2;      // 0.3
அச்சு 99.99 * 3;      // 299.97
அச்சு 18%;            // 0.18
</syntaxhighlight>

A conditional, reading an income and applying a tax rate above a threshold:

<syntaxhighlight lang="text">
எண் வருவாய்;
அச்சு "வருமானம்: ";
உள்ளிடு வருவாய்;
வரி = 20%;

(வருவாய் > 800000) எனில் {
    அச்சு (வருவாய் - 800000) * வரி;
}
இன்றேல் {
    அச்சு "வரி இல்லை";
}
</syntaxhighlight>

Error handling with result values; <code>?</code> returns the failure to the caller:

<syntaxhighlight lang="text">
செயல் வகு(மேல், கீழ்) {
    (கீழ் == 0) எனில் {
        திரும்பு தவறு("பூஜ்ஜியத்தால் வகுக்க முடியாது");
    }
    திரும்பு சரி(மேல் / கீழ்);
}

செயல் சராசரி_விலை(மொத்தம், எண்ணிக்கை) {
    ஒன்று = வகு(மொத்தம், எண்ணிக்கை)?;
    திரும்பு சரி(வட்டமிடு(ஒன்று, 2));
}

அச்சு சராசரி_விலை(1000, 3);   // சரி(333.33)
அச்சு சராசரி_விலை(1000, 0);   // தவறு(பூஜ்ஜியத்தால் வகுக்க முடியாது)
</syntaxhighlight>

A record shape with a method computing a loan's [[equated monthly installment]]:

<syntaxhighlight lang="text">
வடிவம் கடன் {
    எண் அசல்,
    எண் ஆண்டு_வீதம்,
    எண் மாதங்கள்,

    செயல் தவணை(இது) எண் {
        ம = இது.ஆண்டு_வீதம் / 12;
        காரணி = 1;
        எண்ணி = 0;
        (எண்ணி < இது.மாதங்கள்) சுற்று {
            காரணி = காரணி * (1 + ம);
            எண்ணி = எண்ணி + 1;
        }
        திரும்பு வட்டமிடு(இது.அசல் * ம * காரணி / (காரணி - 1), 2);
    }
}

நிலை வீட்டுக்_கடன் = கடன்{அசல்: 500000, ஆண்டு_வீதம்: 9%, மாதங்கள்: 60};
அச்சு "மாதத் தவணை: " & வீட்டுக்_கடன்.தவணை();   // மாதத் தவணை: 10379.18
</syntaxhighlight>

Iteration over an array of records, using the standard library's rupee formatting with [[Indian numbering system|Indian digit grouping]]:

<syntaxhighlight lang="text">
இறக்கு "nUlakam/paNam/paNam.qmz";

செலவுகள் = [
    {"பெயர்": "வாடகை", "தொகை": 18000},
    {"பெயர்": "மின்சாரம்", "தொகை": 2450.50}
];

மொத்தம் = 0;
ஒவ்வொரு பதிவு இல் செலவுகள் {
    அச்சு பதிவு.பெயர் & ": " & ரூபாய்(பதிவு.தொகை);
    மொத்தம் = மொத்தம் + பதிவு.தொகை;
}
அச்சு "மொத்தம்: " & ரூபாய்(மொத்தம்);   // மொத்தம்: ₹20,450.50
</syntaxhighlight>

An HTTP route returning JSON:

<syntaxhighlight lang="text">
வழி பெறு, "/api/hello" {
    ஜேசான்_உரை "{\"message\": \"Hello, World!\"}", 200;
}

வழங்கி_தொடங்கு "localhost", 8080;
</syntaxhighlight>

== Libraries ==
eTamil's standard library, ''nUlakam'' (from {{lang|ta|நூலகம்}}, "library"), is written in eTamil rather than in the host language.<ref name="nulakam" /> Its general-purpose modules cover strings, mathematics, arrays, records, dates and working-day calendars, [[JSON]], [[Base64]] and hexadecimal encoding, document templating, and a unit-testing framework whose failures produce a non-zero exit status for use in [[continuous integration]].<ref name="readme" /><ref name="nulakam" />

Its domain modules include:<ref name="readme" /><ref name="nulakam" />
* '''Accounting''' – a chart of accounts, journal and ledger with a balance check, [[Goods and Services Tax (India)|Goods and Services Tax]] entries, accounting periods, multi-entity and foreign-currency handling, period close, and the [[trial balance]], [[income statement]], [[balance sheet]] and [[cash flow statement]].
* '''Depreciation, payroll and tax''' – straight-line and written-down-value [[depreciation]], payroll, and a marginal [[tax bracket|slab]] engine for income tax and professional tax. Rates are held in an effective-dated table that is queried by date; the library ships with no rates.
* '''Cost accounting''' – cost sheets, overhead absorption, [[standard costing]] variances, [[cost–volume–profit analysis]], process costing and [[activity-based costing]].
* '''Revenue recognition''' – the five steps of Ind AS 115, the Indian counterpart of [[IFRS 15]].
* '''Project control''' – work breakdown structures, [[earned value management|earned value]], the [[critical path method|critical path]], risk registers and [[three-point estimation]].
* '''Banking, insurance and customs''' – simple and compound interest, loan installments, asset classification, insurance claim settlement including the average clause, and the customs duty cascade.
* '''Retail banking''' – loans, with repayment allocation, day-count conventions, floating-rate loans, amortisation of fees by the effective interest method, modification, prepayment, restructuring and write-off; deposits, including term and recurring deposits, the life of an account, and tax deducted at source on interest; payment mandates for UPI AutoPay and the National Automated Clearing House; [[Know your customer|KYC]] status and consent; the lending side of a [[credit bureau]]; a client for [[Aadhaar]] e-signature; and a mapping from banking events to balanced general-ledger entries. Rates and thresholds are supplied as parameters rather than held in the libraries.<ref name="release-2" /><ref name="nulakam" />
* '''Payments and ledgers''' – [[Unified Payments Interface]] address validation, payment links and a payment state machine, and a client for [[Hyperledger]] Fabric.

The runtime also provides database drivers for [[SQLite]], [[PostgreSQL]], [[MySQL]] and [[MariaDB]], [[MongoDB]] and [[Redis]], in which queries are always parameterised;<ref name="roadmap" /><ref name="readme" /> outbound HTTP; [[HMAC]]-SHA256, [[Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm|ECDSA]] P-256, [[bcrypt]] and [[JSON Web Token]] support; mutual [[Transport Layer Security|TLS]]; and reading and writing [[OpenDocument]] and [[Office Open XML]] files.<ref name="readme" />

== Development environments ==
The reference implementation includes an interactive [[read–eval–print loop]], started with <code>--repl</code>.<ref name="readme" /> A [[Visual Studio Code]] extension provides syntax highlighting for every keyword spelling, completions for the built-in and standard-library functions, and diagnostics as the programmer types; it bundles the compiler, standard library, examples and the eTamil typeface, so that installing it installs the language.<ref name="readme" /><ref name="vscode" /> The extension's grammar is generated from the compiler's lexer.<ref name="readme" /> The language server, ''etamil-lsp'', implements the [[Language Server Protocol]] and is used by plugins for [[JetBrains]] IDEs and [[Visual Studio]] and by an [[Emacs]] major mode, and the Visual Studio Code extension is also distributed through Open VSX, the registry used by forks of that editor. A kernel for [[Project Jupyter]] runs eTamil in notebooks.<ref name="release-2" />

Grammars are also maintained for [[Tree-sitter (parser generator)|tree-sitter]], Pygments, Rouge and highlight.js. The project's website hosts an in-browser editor built on a [[WebAssembly]] build of the compiler,<ref name="android" /> and a bilingual user manual in English and Tamil.<ref name="manual" />

== Implementations ==
=== Reference implementation ===
The reference implementation, <code>etamil</code>, is a single Rust crate. Source text is tokenised by a lexer generated with the ''logos'' library, parsed into an [[abstract syntax tree]] and checked, then executed by one of two back ends.<ref name="readme" /> The primary back end compiles to bytecode for a virtual machine, which runs every feature of the language and also serves as the HTTP server, using either a thread pool or an [[Asynchronous I/O|asynchronous]] accept loop built on the Tokio library.<ref name="readme" /><ref name="roadmap" /> The second back end emits [[LLVM]] [[intermediate representation]] on Linux and macOS.<ref name="llvm" /> Since version 1.3.0 the same LLVM route can also compile a program to firmware for Arduino boards.<ref name="changelog" />

Prebuilt packages are published for Windows (x86-64), Linux (x86-64 and ARM64, as statically linked [[musl]] binaries) and macOS (x86-64 and [[Apple silicon]]); installation does not require administrator rights.<ref name="readme" /><ref name="changelog" /> From version 2.0.0 the compiler is also distributed through [[Homebrew (package manager)|Homebrew]], an [[APT (software)|apt]] repository and [[RPM Package Manager|RPM]] packages, as a [[Docker (software)|Docker]] image, and through [[npm]] and [[NuGet]].<ref name="release-2" />

=== Limitations of the reference implementation ===
The LLVM back end represents every value as a handle into a run-time arena and calls into the same Rust library for each operation. This keeps its decimal results identical to the virtual machine's, but compiled programs must link that library, and statements performing file, database or HTTP operations are not yet supported by it.<ref name="readme" /><ref name="llvm" /> The virtual machine itself is synchronous and has no user-level threads.<ref name="arch" /> A program can hold only one database connection at a time, and there is no language-level transaction construct.<ref name="roadmap" />

=== Other implementations ===
An [[Android (operating system)|Android]] application embeds the reference compiler, cross-compiled and loaded through the [[Java Native Interface]], and runs the complete language including the virtual machine, SQLite and the HTTP client.<ref name="android" /> The in-browser editor uses a WebAssembly build of the compiler's front end.<ref name="android" />

=== Performance ===
In measurements published by the project in September 2026, an eTamil HTTP server used about 3.5&nbsp;MB of resident memory, and four demonstration backends together used 14&nbsp;MB, on a virtual server with 4&nbsp;GB of memory and two virtual CPUs.<ref name="mvp" />

== Language development ==
eTamil is developed in the open on [[GitHub]] under the AGPL-3.0-or-later licence.<ref name="citation" /> Its roadmap divides the work into five phases taken from the language's design paper; as of September 2026 the compiler and core language were described as substantially complete, and the open-source release had been made, but no pilot deployment against a production system had taken place.<ref name="roadmap" /> Continuous integration runs on two operating systems, and checks that the editor grammars and published keyword counts match the compiler.<ref name="roadmap" /><ref name="readme" />

The project maintains a narrow layering rule: a capability is implemented in the Rust host only when it cannot be expressed in eTamil. Rounding, which needs the decimal representation, is built in; Indian digit grouping, which is string manipulation, is written in eTamil.<ref name="arch" />

== Naming ==
Standard-library modules are named with romanized Tamil words, such as <code>paNam.qmz</code> ({{lang|ta|பணம்}}, "money") and <code>kaNakkiyal</code> ({{lang|ta|கணக்கியல்}}, "accounting").<ref name="nulakam" />

